    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0192",
      "sequence": 204,
      "meaning": "The compassionate, tender-hearted Lord appears for Kauśalyā’s good. His wondrous form delights the mother and steals sages’ minds: cloud-dark body, lovely eyes, four arms with his own weapons, ornaments, forest garland, vast eyes, an ocean of beauty and foe of Khara. Joining her hands, Kauśalyā asks how she can praise the infinite Lord whom Vedas and Purāṇas declare beyond māyā, qualities, knowledge and measure—the ocean of compassion and joy, repository of every virtue—who has manifested for her out of love for devotees. Scripture says countless universes made by māyā dwell in every pore of him, so the thought that he dwells in her womb seems astonishing. Smiling at her realization, the Lord explains his play so that she may love him as a son. She asks him to abandon the divine form and perform sweet infant play; the all-wise Lord becomes a crying baby. Those who sing this deed attain Hari’s feet and do not fall into worldly existence.",
