    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0195",
      "sequence": 207,
      "meaning": "Kaikeyī and Sumitrā also give birth to two beautiful sons, and neither Sarasvatī nor the serpent king can describe that hour’s joy, splendour and gathering. Ayodhyā seems like Night herself come to meet the Lord: seeing the Sun she appears bashful, yet takes the form of twilight. Clouds of aloeswood incense become darkness, flying red powder a crimson glow, jewel-filled temples the stars, and the king’s palace-vessels the radiant moon; soft Vedic chanting in the houses resembles birds calling at evening. Enraptured by the celebration, the sun loses track of time: a month becomes a day, and the exhausted sun stands still with chariot and asks how night can come.",
